 \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe prove the statement for a general integer \\(n\\ge 1\\) and then substitute \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each of the \\(n\\) rows must contain exactly one uncovered unit square and each of the \\(n\\) columns must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a set  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nConversely, any permutation \\(\\sigma\\) yields a configuration satisfying the requirement.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Decomposition into left and right regions  \n\nDefine  \n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\nThus \\(L\\) consists of the squares strictly to the left of the uncovered square in their row, and \\(R\\) consists of the squares strictly to the right.  \nClearly  \n\\[\n\\text{covered squares}=L\\cup R,\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Any rectangle lies wholly in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\)  \n\nLet \\(T\\) be an axis‑aligned rectangle whose interior contains only covered squares.  \nAssume that \\(T\\) contains two squares \\((i,j_1)\\in L\\) and \\((i,j_2)\\in R\\) belonging to the same row \\(i\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) is a rectangle, it also contains every square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j_1\\le j\\le j_2\\); in particular it contains \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), which is uncovered – a contradiction.  \nHence a rectangle cannot contain squares on both sides of the hole in any row, and therefore \\(T\\subseteq L\\) or \\(T\\subseteq R\\).\n\nConsequently the tiling problem separates into two independent subproblems: tile \\(L\\) and tile \\(R\\) with rectangles.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Structure of \\(L\\) and \\(R\\)  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) the number of squares of \\(L\\) in that row equals \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),