rmutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i>1\\) place a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) covering the squares\n\\[\n(i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1).\n\\]\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i<n\\) place a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) covering the squares\n\\[\n(i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n).\n\\]\n\nThese rectangles are pairwise disjoint (they lie in distinct rows and on opposite sides of the diagonal) and together cover all \\(n^2-n\\) covered squares.  \nThe number of rectangles used is\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus the lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n#### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nThe minimal possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n---\n\n#### 10.  Remark on the case of zero tiles  \n\nIf no tile is placed, every unit square remains uncovered. Consequently each row (and each column) would contain \\(2025\\) uncovered squares, violating the requirement that there be **exactly one** uncovered square per row and per column. Therefore the configuration with zero tiles does **not** satisfy the constraints."
