{c=2}^{n}\\operatorname{comp}(T_c)\n   \\;\\ge\\;2(n-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1), (2) and (4) we obtain the universal lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nL+R\\;\\ge\\;2n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nThus **any** admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the *identity* permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(i)=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n),\n\\]\n\nso the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left side.*  \nFor each column \\(c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,c+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal{L}_c\\) is a \\((n-c)\\times1\\) rectangle; it lies completely to the left of\nthe diagonal hole in every row it occupies, hence it is allowed.  \nThe family \\(\\{\\mathcal{L}_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) tiles and covers\nall cells with column index smaller than the row index.\n\n*Right side.*  \nFor each column \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{R}_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal{R}_c\\) is a \\((c-1)\\times1\\) rectangle; it lies completely to the right\nof the diagonal hole in every row it occupies.  \nThe family \\(\\{\\mathcal{R}_c\\}_{c=2}^{n}\\) also contains \\(n-1\\) tiles and covers\nall cells with column index larger than the row index.\n\nThe two families are disjoint, none of the tiles contains a diagonal square,\nand together they cover every non‑diagonal cell of the board.  Hence we have a\nvalid tiling with exactly\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Optimality\n\nThe lower bound proved in Section 2 shows that no tiling can use fewer than\n\\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction of Section 3 achieves exactly this\nnumber.  Therefore the bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of\ntiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Explicit example for the smallest non‑zero case  \n\nWhen \\(n=2\\) the identity permutation leaves the squares \\((1,1)\\) and\n\\((2,2)\\) uncovered.  The optimal tiling consists of the two vertical\n\\(1\\times1\\) tiles  \n\n\\[\n\\{(2,1)\\},\\qquad \\{(1,2)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the pattern described above for \\(n=2\\) (two tiles, i.e.\n\\(2\\cdot2-2=2\\)).  This illustrates the construction in the smallest\nnon‑trivial instance.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe minimum number of rectangular tiles required for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid,\nwith exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column, is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
