) those to the right.  Set  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\nThe squares that must be covered are precisely \\(L\\cup R\\); the two sets are\ndisjoint.  Any axis\u2011parallel rectangle (tile) cannot contain a hole, therefore\nit lies wholly inside \\(L\\) or wholly inside \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (left region)\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=p(i)-1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_i\\) is the column of the rightmost square of \\(L_i\\).  \nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, the numbers \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) are exactly\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); consequently there are \\(n-1\\) rows for which\n\\(\\ell_i\\ge1\\).\n\nConsider any tiling of \\(L\\) by rectangles.  Take a row \\(i\\) with\n\\(\\ell_i\\ge1\\).  The square \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) must belong to some rectangle \\(R\\).\nSince the adjacent square \\((i,\\ell_i+1)=(i,p(i))\\) is a hole, \\(R\\) cannot extend\nto column \\(\\ell_i+1\\); therefore the **rightmost column of \\(R\\) is exactly\n\\(\\ell_i\\)**.  Thus each such row forces a rectangle whose rightmost column equals a\ndistinct value \\(\\ell_i\\).  A rectangle has only one rightmost column, so it can\nserve at most one of the \\(n-1\\) rows.  Hence any tiling of \\(L\\) uses at least\n\\(n-1\\) rectangles. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (right region)\n\nDefine for each row  \n\n\\[\nr_i:=n-p(i),\n\\]\n\nthe number of squares of \\(R_i\\).  The set \\(\\{r_i\\}\\) is again\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  For a row with \\(r_i\\ge1\\) the leftmost square of\n\\(R_i\\) is \\((i,p(i)+1)\\).  By the same reasoning as in Lemma\u202f1, any rectangle\ncovering this square must have **leftmost column equal to \\(p(i)+1\\)**.  Since the\nvalues \\(p(i)+1\\) (equivalently the \\(r_i\\)) are distinct, at least \\(n-1\\)\nrectangles are required to cover \\(R\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary (global lower bound)\n\nAny admissible tiling of the whole board must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction achieving the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  The uncovered squares\nare the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) define two rectangles:\n\n\\[\nA_k=\\{(i,j):k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\},\n\\qquad\nB_k=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n* \\(A_k\\) has dimensions \\((n-k)\\times k\\) and lies strictly **below** the\n  diagonal, so it contains no uncovered square.\n* \\(B_k\\) has dimensions \\(k\\times (n-k)\\) and lies strictly **above** the\n  diagonal, also avoiding uncovered squares.\n* The families \\(\\{A_k\\}\\) and \\(\\{B_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint:\n  - Different \\(A_k\\) have disjoint column sets \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n  - Different \\(B_k\\) have disjoint row sets \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n  - No \\(A_k\\) intersects any \\(B_{k'}\\) because rows of \\(A_k\\) are\n    \\(>k\\) while columns of \\(B_{k'}\\) are \\(>k'\\).\n\nEvery off\u2011diagonal square is covered exactly once:\n* If \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in A_j\\) (take \\(k=j\\)).\n* If \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in B_i\\) (take \\(k=i\\)).\n\nThus the \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles \\(\\{A_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{B_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\)\ncover all squares except the diagonal, satisfying the requirement with exactly\n\\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Minimality\n\nLemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 give the lower bound \\(2n-2\\); the construction above meets\nthis bound.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
