e set \\(U\\) of uncovered squares is a set of \\(n\\) unit squares with the property that no two lie in the same row or the same column.  \nThus \\(U\\) is the graph of a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\([n]\\):\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\}.\n\\]\n\nRenaming the columns by the permutation \\(\\pi^{-1}\\) does not change the tiling problem, because the grid is symmetric under column permutations.  \nHence we may assume \\(\\pi\\) is the identity; i.e. the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal squares\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i\\in[n]\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Matrix formulation  \n\nDefine the \\(n\\times n\\) matrix\n\\[\nA=J-I,\n\\]\nwhere \\(J\\) is the all\u2011ones matrix and \\(I\\) is the identity matrix.  \nFor \\(i,j\\in[n]\\) we have\n\\[\nA_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1, & i\\neq j,\\\\[2pt]\n0, & i=j .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThus the entry \\(A_{ij}=1\\) exactly when the unit square \\((i,j)\\) must be covered by a tile.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Tiles as rank\u20111 matrices  \n\nA rectangular tile occupies a set of rows \\(R\\subseteq[n]\\) and a set of columns \\(C\\subseteq[n]\\) with \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\) (otherwise a diagonal square would be covered).  \nLet \\(u\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}\\) be the characteristic vector of \\(R\\) and \\(v\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}\\) the characteristic vector of \\(C\\).  \nThe tile covers precisely the entries \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\); algebraically this is the rank\u20111 matrix\n\\[\nT=uv^{\\mathsf T},\\qquad (T)_{ij}=u_i v_j .\n\\]\n\nBecause the tiles are pairwise disjoint, the sum of their matrices equals \\(A\\):\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{m} u^{(k)}\\bigl(v^{(k)}\\bigr)^{\\mathsf T},\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(m\\) is the number of tiles and each summand has rank \\(1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound via rank  \n\nThe rank of a sum of matrices does not exceed the sum of the ranks:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\operatorname{rank}\\bigl(u^{(k)}(v^{(k)})^{\\mathsf T}\\bigr)\n= \\sum_{k=1}^{m