-----------------------------------\n### 4.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the **identity permutation** \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen \\(L_i=i-1\\) and \\(R_i=n-i\\).  \n\n*For the left region.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n-1)\\) place a vertical tile of size \\((n-k)\\times1\\) covering rows \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\) in column \\(k\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every cell of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*For the right region.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\;(2\\le k\\le n)\\) place a vertical tile of size \\((k-1)\\times1\\) covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,k-1\\) in column \\(k\\).  \nAgain we obtain \\(n-1\\) disjoint tiles covering all of \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\nThe two families together consist of exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.  \nEvery row \\(i\\) contains the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\); every column \\(j\\) contains the uncovered square \\((j,j)\\).  \nThus the placement satisfies the problem\u2019s requirement and uses \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Minimality  \n\nEquation (15) shows that no placement can use fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, while the construction above uses exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
