diagram into rectangles (with no overlapping) is equal to the number of \"corners\" or something like that. Actually, there is a known result: any Young diagram can be partitioned into exactly the number of rows (or columns) rectangles by using horizontal strips (each row as a rectangle). But you can also partition into fewer rectangles by merging rows if they have same length, but here lengths are decreasing, so you cannot merge rows because they have different lengths. However, you could use vertical strips: each column j has j-1 squares in the upper triangle. So you could also partition using vertical strips: column j (for j=2,...,n) has j-1 squares. So you could use n-1 vertical strips. So the minimal number is at least the size of the largest antichain in the poset of squares? Let's think.\n\nThe shape is a monotone shape: each row length is decreasing by 1. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition this shape is indeed n-1, because each rectangle must be a product of a set of rows and a set of columns. If you try to cover more than one row with a rectangle, you must also include columns that are common to those rows. But the columns sets for rows i and i+1 are [i+1,n] and [i+2,n] respectively. The intersection is [i+2,n], which is a subset. So you could create a rectangle covering rows i,i+1 and columns i+2,...,n. This would cover squares (i,i+2..n) and (i+1,i+2..n). But then squares (i,i+1) and (i+1,i+2) would be left uncovered. Actually, (i,i+1) is not covered by that rectangle because column i+1 is not included. So you would need to cover that square separately. So you could reduce the number of rectangles by merging some rows, but you would need to cover the squares that are not covered by the merged rectangle with other rectangles. Could that reduce the total count? Possibly you could cover the shape with fewer than n-1 rectangles by using a combination of horizontal and vertical rectangles. But the shape is a Ferrers diagram with a \"staircase