n\n   an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle lying below the diagonal (note that \\(V_1\\) covers the whole column\u202f1 below the diagonal, fixing the omission pointed out in the bug report).\n\n   The two families are pairwise disjoint, avoid all diagonal squares, and together cover every off\u2011diagonal square exactly once.\n\n5. **Counting tiles.**  \n   There are \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles and \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles, totalling  \n\n   \\[\n   2(n-1)=2n-2=4048\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\nSince the lower bound (1) is achieved, it is optimal. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the rows and columns of the grid by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Form of the uncovered squares  \n\nEach row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nTherefore the set \\(U\\) of uncovered squares consists of \\(n\\) squares, no two sharing a row or a column; consequently there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe grid is symmetric under a permutation of its columns: permuting the columns does not change the nature of the problem (tiles remain rectangles with sides on grid lines, and the condition \u201cone uncovered square per row and column\u2019\u2019 is preserved).  \nApplying the column permutation \\(\\sigma=\\pi^{-1}\\) to the whole board transforms the set (2) into  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe main diagonal.  The number of tiles is unchanged by this global column permutation.  Hence, **without loss of generality we may assume that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Geometry of a tile that avoids the diagonal  \n\nA tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; it is uniquely described by a row interval \\([a,b]\\) and a column interval \\([c,d]\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le b\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le c\\le d\\le n.\n\\]\n\nThe tile consists of all unit squares \\((r,s)\\) satisfying \\(a\\le r\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le s\\le d\\).\n\nIf the tile contains a diagonal square \\((k,k)\\), then \\(k\\in[a,b]\\cap[c,d]\\).  \nConversely, if \\([a,b]\\cap[c,d]\\neq\\varnothing\\), pick any \\(k\\) in the intersection; then \\((k,k)\\) belongs to the tile.  \nThus **a tile avoids all diagonal squares iff the two intervals are disjoint**, i.e.\n\n\\[\n[a,b]\\cap[c,d]=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe only possibilities for disjoint intervals are  \n\n\\[\nb<c\\qquad\\text{(the rectangle lies strictly above the diagonal)},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nor  \n\n\\[\nd<a\\qquad\\text{(the rectangle lies strictly below the diagonal)}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence every admissible tile belongs either to the upper\u2011triangular region  \n\n\\[\nU_T=\\{(r,s)\\mid r<s\\},\n\\]\n\nor to the lower\u2011triangular region  \n\n\\[\nL_T=\\{(r,s)\\mid r>s\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\n##### 3.1  Upper\u2011triangular part  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nS_U=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subseteq U_T .\n\\]\n\nLet \\(R\\) be any tile satisfying (4) (i.e. lying above the diagonal) with row interval \\([a,b]\\) and column interval \\([c,d]\\).  \nSuppose \\(R\\) contains two distinct squares \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) from \\(S_U\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nFrom \\((i,i+1)\\in R\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le i\\le b<c\\le i+1\\le d .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b<c\\) and \\(i\\le b\\), (6) forces \\(i+1\\le c\\).  Together with \\(c\\le i+1\\) (the column of \\((i,i+1)\\) belongs to \\([c,d]\\)) we obtain \\(c=i+1\\).  \nApplying the same reasoning to \\((j,j+1)\\in R\\) yields \\(c=j+1\\).  Hence \\(i+1=j+1\\), i.e. \\(i=j\\), a contradiction.  \n\nThus **any tile above the diagonal contains at most one element of \\(S_U\\)**.  \nSince \\(|S_U|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover the whole upper\u2011triangular region.\n\n##### 3.2  Lower\u2011triangular part  \n\nBy symmetry consider  \n\n\\[\nS_L=\\{(i,i-1)\\mid i=2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\subseteq L_T .\n\\]\n\nA tile below the diagonal satisfies (5); an argument identical to the one above (now using the column\u2011end \\(d\\) instead of the column\u2011start) shows that such a tile can contain at most one element of \\(S_L\\).  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for the lower\u2011triangular region.\n\n##### 3.3  Combined lower bound  \n\nEvery admissible tile belongs either to the upper\u2011triangular or to the lower\u2011triangular region, never both.  Hence the total number \\(m\\) of tiles satisfies  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nDefine two families of rectangles.\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle.  \nIts row interval is \\([i,i]\\) and its column interval is \\([i+1,n]\\); therefore \\(b=i<c=i+1\\), so \\(H_i\\) lies strictly above the diagonal and does **not** contain any diagonal square.\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle.  \nIts row interval is \\([j+1,n]\\) and its column interval is \\([j,j]\\); thus \\(d=j<a=j+1\\), so \\(V_j\\) lies strictly below the diagonal and avoids all diagonal squares.  \nIn particular, \\(V_1\\) covers the squares \\((2,1),(3,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\), fixing the omission highlighted in the bug report.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Verification of the construction  \n\n1. **Rectangularity.**  \n   Both \\(H_i\\) and \\(V_j\\) are Cartesian products of consecutive rows and consecutive columns, hence are rectangles whose sides lie on grid lines.\n\n2. **No diagonal square is covered.**  \n   - For any \\(H_i\\) the column indices are all \\(\\ge i+1\\), while the diagonal square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,i)\\) with column \\(i\\); thus \\((i,i)\\notin H_i\\).  \n   - For any \\(V_j\\) the row indices are all \\(\\ge j+1\\), while the diagonal square in column \\(j\\) is \\((j,j)\\) with row \\(j\\); thus \\((j,j)\\notin V_j\\).  \n   Hence every diagonal square \\((k,k)\\) remains uncovered.\n\n3. **Pairwise disjointness.**  \n   - Distinct horizontal tiles \\(H_i\\) and \\(H_{i'}\\) occupy different rows (\\(i\\neq i'\\)), so they cannot intersect.  \n   - Distinct vertical tiles \\(V_j\\) and \\(V_{j'}\\) occupy different columns (\\(j\\neq j'\\)), so they cannot intersect.  \n   - Suppose a horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) and a vertical tile \\(V_j\\) intersect. Then a common square would have coordinates \\((i,j)\\).  \n     From \\(H_i\\) we have \\(j\\ge i+1\\); from \\(V_j\\) we have \\(i\\ge j+1\\).  \n     These two inequalities cannot hold simultaneously, so no such intersection exists.  \n   Therefore the whole family \\(\\{H_i\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\}\\) consists of pairwise disjoint tiles.\n\n4. **Coverage of all off\u2011diagonal squares.**  \n   Let \\((r,s)\\) be a unit square with \\(r\\neq s\\).  \n   - If \\(r<s\\) (the square lies above the diagonal), then \\(s\\in\\{r+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently \\((r,s)\\in H_r\\).  \n   - If \\(r>s\\) (the square lies below the diagonal), then \\(r\\in\\{s+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently \\((r,s)\\in V_s\\).  \n   Hence every off\u2011diagonal square belongs to exactly one tile.\n\n5. **Number of tiles.**  \n   There are \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) and \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\).  \n   Thus the total number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (7) shows that any admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nThe construction above uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the lower bound is attainable and is optimal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nThe smallest possible number of rectangular tiles that can be placed on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid while leaving exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
